<article class="post-body">

<section id="s-cliff" data-code="cliff">
<div class="section-no">01 — Acceptance Rate Cliff</div>
<h2>DeepSeek-V4's MTP Falls Off a Cliff</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding lets a cheap drafter propose multiple tokens that the expensive target model verifies in one parallel pass. The attainable speedup depends critically on <em>how many consecutive draft tokens are accepted</em> — the average accepted length τ. A high τ at Pos0 that crashes by Pos3 is nearly useless.</p>
<p>DeepSeek-V4 ships a Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) module as its native drafter. MTP is autoregressive: it predicts Pos1 conditioned on the anchor, then predicts Pos2 conditioned on its own Pos1 prediction (unverified), and so on. Each unverified intermediate token is a potential error — and errors compound. The result is a sharp positional degradation curve that limits τ to roughly 2.93 at the standard 3-step configuration.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Per-Position Acceptance Rate — MTP vs HyperDFlash (non-thinking, T=0)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Hover over the chart</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-legend">
      <div class="legend-item"><div class="legend-dot" style="background:#f59e0b"></div>Native MTP — collapses after Pos1</div>
      <div class="legend-item"><div class="legend-dot" style="background:#8b5cf6"></div>HyperDFlash — smooth profile maintained</div>
    </div>
    <canvas id="cliff-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The chart above shows exactly why MTP's 6-step deployment (MTP(6)) is <em>slower</em> than the 3-step version despite seeing more draft tokens. By Pos5, MTP acceptance drops below 20% — those extra positions cost verification overhead with almost no accepted output to show for it. The speedup formula S = τ / (1 + γc) means a low τ and high verification cost γc makes more steps actively harmful.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-block" data-code="block">
<div class="section-no">02 — Block Parallel Drafting</div>
<h2>DFlash: Draft an Entire Block at Once</h2>
<p>DFlash (Block Diffusion for Flash Speculative Decoding) takes a fundamentally different approach: instead of predicting each position conditioned on the previous (unverified) draft, it predicts all positions in the block simultaneously from the verified anchor context. No error accumulation — every draft position sees the same clean, verified anchor hidden state.</p>
<p>The block input uses mask tokens at ungenerated positions. The drafter attends to the anchor's hidden state and the masked block, then fills in all positions in one forward pass. The target model then verifies all draft tokens in parallel and accepts the longest matching prefix.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Block Parallel Drafting — Four-Step Cycle</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Walk through each step</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="block-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target produces anchor hidden state</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The target model processes the full verified context up to position <em>a</em>, producing a rich hidden state h<sub>a</sub> that encodes all preceding verified tokens.</div>
          <div class="step-code">h_a = target(context[0:a])  # verified, clean</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Drafter fills block with mask tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A lightweight drafter sees h<sub>a</sub> and [MASK, MASK, …, MASK] at positions a+1…a+6. It predicts all 6 draft tokens in a single forward pass — no conditioning on unverified intermediate predictions.</div>
          <div class="step-code">draft = drafter(h_a, [MASK]*6)  # parallel, no error chain</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target verifies all draft tokens in parallel</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The target model runs one forward pass over [context, draft[0..5]], comparing its predictions to each draft token. This is the key efficiency gain — one verification pass for up to 6 accepted tokens.</div>
          <div class="step-code">accepted = target.verify(draft)  # one pass, up to 6 tokens</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accept prefix, advance anchor</div>
          <div class="step-desc">All tokens up to the first mismatch are accepted. The new anchor advances by τ positions (avg 3.69 for HyperDFlash). Next cycle begins from the new anchor.</div>
          <div class="step-code">a += τ  # avg 3.69 tokens accepted per round</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="block-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="block-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="block-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Block drafting eliminates the autoregressive error chain entirely: every position in the block sees the same verified anchor context. The challenge is that doing this correctly requires the drafter to be well-conditioned on the target's hidden state — and for DeepSeek-V4, that hidden state has an unusual structure.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-mhc" data-code="mhc">
<div class="section-no">03 — The MHC Architecture Problem</div>
<h2>Why DeepSeek-V4's MHC Breaks Vanilla DFlash</h2>
<p>DeepSeek-V4 uses <strong>Multi-Hyper-Connection (MHC)</strong> residual streams. Instead of maintaining a single hidden state vector per token, each token is represented by <em>m parallel residual paths</em> that run through the entire network. These paths are only collapsed into a single vector immediately before the LM head via the model's built-in <code>hc_head</code> module.</p>
<p>The <code>hc_head</code> collapse is input-dependent: it computes one scalar sigmoid gate per path, weighting each path's contribution based on the token's context. This is fundamentally different from a fixed linear projection.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">MHC Path Collapse (hc_head)</div>
  x̃<sub>t</sub> = RMSNorm(vec(H<sub>t</sub>))<br>
  α<sub>t</sub> = σ(W<sub>f</sub> x̃<sub>t</sub> + b) &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># per-path sigmoid gates</span><br>
  y<sub>t</sub> = Σ<sub>j=1..m</sub> α<sub>t,j</sub> · H<sub>t,j</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># weighted sum of m paths</span>
</div>

<p>When a generic DFlash drafter tries to adapt to DeepSeek-V4, it faces two problems. First, it typically conditions on intermediate-layer aggregated features — but those features have already lost the multi-path structure the target relies on. Second, to collapse the multi-path residual into a single conditioning vector, it uses a dense learned projection of 67M parameters — a static linear map that cannot express the input-dependent gating the target model uses.</p>

<div class="compare-grid">
  <div class="compare-card bad">
    <div class="compare-card-title">❌ Vanilla DFlash Adaptation</div>
    <p>Uses intermediate-layer aggregated features as conditioning. Multi-path structure is gone before the drafter even sees it.</p>
    <p>Collapses MHC paths with a <strong>dense static linear projection</strong> — 67M random parameters, learned from scratch, structurally misaligned with the target's own gating.</p>
    <span class="param-count">67M params — random init</span>
  </div>
  <div class="compare-card good">
    <div class="compare-card-title">✓ HyperDFlash Design</div>
    <p>Conditions on the <strong>pre-collapse MHC residual</strong> — the same multi-path representation the target's LM head actually sees.</p>
    <p>Collapses paths with the <strong>same gated form as hc_head</strong>, initialized directly from the target's pretrained weights — inherently aligned, no structural mismatch.</p>
    <span class="param-count">65K params — inherited init</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The parameter difference is stark: vanilla DFlash needs a 67M-parameter projection to imperfectly collapse the multi-path residual. HyperDFlash's Inherited HC-Gate Reducer uses exactly 65K parameters — a 1000× reduction — because it reuses the same functional form as the target's own hc_head. The alignment is structural, not just numerical.</p>

