<section id="s-design" data-code="design">
<div class="section-no">04 — Three Innovations</div>
<h2>HyperDFlash's Three-Part Fix</h2>
<p>HyperDFlash combines three targeted innovations, each addressing a specific alignment gap between block speculative drafting and DeepSeek-V4's MHC architecture.</p>

<h3>1. Pre-Collapse Residual Conditioning</h3>
<p>Rather than conditioning the drafter on aggregated intermediate layer features, HyperDFlash uses the target model's <strong>final pre-collapse MHC residual states</strong> as the conditioning signal. These are the same multi-path representations that feed into the target's LM head — the most informative conditioning signal possible, already computed by the target during its verification pass. No extra computation required: the pre-collapse residual is cached for MTP and reused directly.</p>

<h3>2. Inherited HC-Gate Reducer</h3>
<p>The pre-collapse residual has shape [m × d] (four paths × hidden dim). The drafter needs a single conditioning vector of shape [d]. Vanilla DFlash learns a dense 67M-parameter projection to do this collapse. HyperDFlash instead <strong>inherits the functional form of the target's own hc_head</strong>: a lightweight gated aggregation with per-path RMSNorm and sigmoid gates. The gate parameters are initialized directly from the pretrained target hc_head weights.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Reducer Comparison — Parameter Count vs Structural Alignment</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="reducer-canvas" style="width:100%;height:160px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>3. Targeted KL Distillation</h3>
<p>Standard cross-entropy training uses one-hot labels — it tells the model which token won, but not the full probability distribution the target assigns to other candidates. For early draft positions (k=1 and k=2), the teacher model can provide meaningful soft supervision: at k=1, both teacher and student condition on the same verified context, so the teacher's distribution is directly applicable. HyperDFlash adds a KL divergence loss term (weight α ≈ 0.1–0.2) at these early positions to capture this richer signal.</p>
<p>For k≥3, the teacher conditions on ground-truth intermediate tokens while the drafter sees only mask tokens — the distributions diverge, so KL distillation is deliberately not applied to avoid high-variance conflicting gradients.</p>
</section>

