    <h2>Audio Summary: HyperDFlash — MHC-Aligned Block Speculative Decoding</h2>
    <p>HyperDFlash is a speculative decoding framework designed for DeepSeek-V4, which uses an unusual multi-hyper-connection architecture where each token is represented by four parallel residual paths instead of a single hidden state. The key problem it solves: DeepSeek-V4's native MTP drafter works by predicting tokens sequentially, conditioning each position on unverified previous draft tokens. This causes acceptance rates to collapse sharply after the first two positions, limiting practical speedup.</p>
    <p>HyperDFlash instead drafts an entire block of six tokens in parallel from the verified anchor context, eliminating error accumulation. Three innovations make this work on the MHC architecture: it conditions on the pre-collapse multi-path residual rather than aggregated intermediate features, it collapses the multi-path residual using the same gated functional form as the target model's own hc-head (65,000 parameters versus 67 million for a generic projection), and it adds KL distillation at early draft positions. Result: average accepted length of 3.69 and 2.80x decoding speedup, outperforming both native MTP and vanilla DFlash across all seven math, code, and chat benchmarks tested.</p>
